<HelpTab>
    <FluentText>
        <a href="/files">Files</a> holds your company's images, videos, music and documents: posters, party
        artwork, projection videos, course pictures and anything else you want to keep in one place.
        Admins and people with the CanManageFiles role see everything and can change it. Everyone else sees
        <strong>Shared files</strong>: the folders that have been shared with staff, which they can look at and
        download but not change.
    </FluentText>
    <FluentText>
        Staff documents such as right-to-work checks, and the sounds behind audio clips, are kept with their
        own features and never appear in Files.
    </FluentText>

    <HelpSection Title="Getting around">
        <FluentText>
            On a computer, the left side has <strong>Quick access</strong> and your folders. <strong>All files</strong>
            is the top level, <strong>Recent</strong> shows the newest uploads, and <strong>Images</strong>,
            <strong>Videos</strong>, <strong>Audio</strong> and <strong>Documents</strong> show every file of that type,
            whichever folder it's in. Select a folder in the tree to open it, or the arrow to show the folders inside it.
        </FluentText>
        <FluentText>
            On a phone, Quick access is the row of buttons under the search box. Tap a folder to open it, and use the
            path above the files (or the up arrow) to go back.
        </FluentText>
        <FluentText>
            The address in your browser follows where you are, so <strong>Back</strong> works, and you can send someone
            a link straight to a folder.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Searching">
        <FluentText>
            Type in <strong>Search all files</strong> to look through the names of files and folders in every folder
            you can see. Each result shows which folder it's in. Press Esc or the cross to go back.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Looking at a file">
        <FluentText>
            Select a file to see its details: a preview, its type and size, who uploaded it and when, and which folder
            it's in. Use <strong>Preview</strong> (or double-click the file) to look at it full size. Images, videos,
            music, PDFs and text files show in the page; use the arrows, or the left and right arrow keys, to step
            through the other files. <strong>Download</strong> saves it to your device.
        </FluentText>
        <FluentText>
            <strong>Grid</strong> and <strong>List</strong> at the top right switch between big previews and a compact
            list, and the menu beside them changes the order. Lanyard remembers both on this device.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>
    <HelpSection Title="Selecting several">
        <FluentText>
            Tick the box in the corner of a file or folder to select it, or hold <strong>Ctrl</strong>
            (<strong>⌘</strong> on a Mac) and click. Hold <strong>Shift</strong> and click to select everything in
            between, and press <strong>Ctrl+A</strong> to select everything shown. On a phone, use a file's
            <strong>⋯</strong> menu and choose <strong>Select</strong>, then tap the others. Press <strong>Esc</strong>
            or the cross to clear the selection.
        </FluentText>
        <FluentText>
            The bar above the files then offers <strong>Download</strong>, which saves everything selected in one
            .zip file (folders included, laid out as they are in Files). A folder's <strong>⋯</strong> menu has
            <strong>Download as .zip</strong> too.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Sharing a link">
        <FluentText>
            <strong>Copy link</strong> (in a file's <strong>⋯</strong> menu or its details) copies a link to the file,
            for a chat message or a training course. Anyone signed in to Lanyard can open it.
        </FluentText>
        <FluentText>
            Files updates by itself when someone else uploads, moves or deletes something, so you always see what's
            there now.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>
</HelpTab>
